<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Nogai Horde</h1><p class="st-meta">1440 CE – 1634 CE (approx.) · capital Saraychik</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A nomadic confederation of the Volga-Ural steppe founded by the emir Edigu's descendants, the main horse-breeding power between Russia and Central Asia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Nogai Horde grew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghud">Mangit tribe</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a>, the emir who dominated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> around 1400. His descendants built a separate power on the steppes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-river">Ural River</a> in the mid-15th century, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraychik">Saraychik</a> on the Ural as their seat.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers held the title bey rather than khan, as they were not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghisid-principle">Genghisids</a>, and governed through a council of mirzas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a> were pastoral nomads whose huge herds of horses were sold to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Muscovy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. They were allies or rivals to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate</a> in turn.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Russian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> and Astrakhan in the 1550s split the horde; the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lesser-nogai-horde">Lesser Nogai Horde</a> moved west under Crimean protection. The rest came under Russian influence, and the arrival of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalmyk-khanate">Kalmyk Khanate</a> in the 1630s drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> steppe.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nogai-horde.jpg" alt="Nogai Horde" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: Ausis; derivative work by Hoodinski · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nogay_Horde.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1440 CE – 1634 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saraychik</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edigu, Ismail Bey, Yusuf Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nogai</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>, <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nogai Khanate, Mangyt Yurt</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1537&amp;focus=nogai-horde" aria-label="Nogai Horde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5889 512 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nogai Horde lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6209 768L6352 637M6209 768L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6352 637h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6209 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6209 768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1537&amp;focus=nogai-horde">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
